    <h2>Why LVP Isn't Always the Answer</h2>

    <p>The pitch is seductive — waterproof, scratch-resistant, cheaper than hardwood, installs fast, looks like wood. So it gets recommended for every room and every problem, and homeowners come to believe LVP is simply the best floor, full stop. It's not a best floor; it's a right-for-certain-jobs floor. Treating it as the universal answer is how people end up covering solid oak with plastic, or getting a bouncy, gapping install because "it just clicks together" made prep sound optional. The material is good. The one-size-fits-all belief around it is the problem.</p>

    <h2>Whatever Goes Down, the Subfloor Decides</h2>

    <p>Whatever goes on top, the floor's success is decided by what's underneath. Floating LVP demands a subfloor flat to within about 3/16 inch over 10 feet — lay it over an unflat, dirty, or damp base and it telegraphs every hump, flexes underfoot, and separates at the seams within a couple of years. On a slab, moisture has to be tested and managed before anything is glued or floated. At a lake house the stakes climb: a floor that isn't acclimated to the house's humidity swings, or laid over the right vapor barrier, expands and contracts through the seasons until it buckles. What goes wrong on the rushed installs is the crew skips the leveling and the moisture check because the customer was told LVP is foolproof — and the "foolproof" floor fails in a way that's entirely about the prep nobody did.</p>

    <h2>What Careful Installers Do First</h2>

    <p>The real work in flooring is the hour before the pretty part. Good installers flatten the subfloor with leveling compound or sanding, clean it to bare, moisture-test a slab, and acclimate the flooring to the house before laying a plank — especially at the lake. And in the older homes near downtown, many of which have original solid hardwood under decades of carpet, the good installer's most valuable move is telling you not to cover it. Solid hardwood can be sanded and refinished five to seven times over its life; refinishing usually costs less than a new floor and restores real character and resale value that a plank of vinyl over the top erases forever. That's where flooring overlaps with a <a href="/locations/cullman/general-contractor/">general contractor</a> on a bigger remodel and with a <a href="/locations/cullman/painter/">painter</a> whose trim work has to meet the new floor cleanly.</p>

    <h2>What a Thin Quote Leaves Out</h2>

    <p>The warning signs are quiet. A quote with no line for subfloor prep or leveling — because a flat subfloor is assumed, not verified. A slab or lake-house job with no mention of moisture testing or acclimation. An installer who reaches for LVP over your existing hardwood without ever raising refinishing, which means they're selling the easy install, not the right floor. The honest admission a good installer offers: sometimes the floor you already have, restored, beats anything you'd buy — and sometimes LVP genuinely is the smart, durable choice for how your family lives or how a lake house gets used. A pro tells you which, even when the more profitable answer for them is the other one.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What Flooring Costs in Cullman</h2>

    <p>Real ranges:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>Luxury vinyl plank</strong> — about $3 to $7 per square foot installed, depending on product and subfloor prep.</li>
      <li><strong>Refinishing existing hardwood</strong> — roughly $3 to $5 per square foot, often cheaper than replacement and restoring more value.</li>
      <li><strong>New solid hardwood</strong> — around $8 to $14 per square foot installed.</li>
      <li><strong>Subfloor prep and moisture control</strong> — leveling, testing, acclimation, and old-floor removal, the line cheap bids leave off.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The number to watch is prep. Two LVP quotes can differ because one includes flattening a wavy subfloor and moisture-proofing a lake house, and the other plans to lay planks and hope.</p>

    <h2>Getting the Material Right</h2>

    <p>Match the material to the room, not the trend: LVP where water and heavy traffic rule, hardwood or refinished hardwood where warmth and value matter, and always ask the installer to walk you through subfloor prep and moisture control before you sign. If your home is one of the older ones and you suspect hardwood under the carpet, have someone look before you buy new flooring — you may already own the best floor in the house. There's no seasonal urgency to flooring, so you have time to get the material choice and the prep right rather than rushing either.</p>

    <h2>Flooring Questions Cullman Homeowners Ask</h2>

    <p><strong>Should I put LVP everywhere?</strong></p>
    <p>Great in kitchens, baths, busy rooms, and lake houses. A poor choice as a cover over original hardwood or an unprepped subfloor. Match it to the room.</p>

    <p><strong>Why is my new floor uneven or gapping?</strong></p>
    <p>The subfloor — and at the lake, moisture. LVP needs it flat to about 3/16 inch over 10 feet, moisture-tested, and acclimated. The plank telegraphs whatever it's laid over.</p>

    <p><strong>What does it cost?</strong></p>
    <p>LVP $3 to $7 a square foot installed; refinishing hardwood $3 to $5; new solid hardwood $8 to $14.</p>

